{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"To solve the problem, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Convert the given rectangular coordinates \\((2, -1, -2)\\) to spherical coordinates \\((\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)\\).\n2. Determine the new spherical coordinates \\((\\rho, \\theta, 2\\phi)\\).\n3. Convert the new spherical coordinates back to rectangular coordinates.\n\n### Step 1: Convert Rectangular Coordinates to Spherical Coordinates\n\nThe formulas to convert from rectangular coordinates \\((x, y, z)\\) to spherical coordinates \\((\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)\\) are:\n\\[\n\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\phi = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}}{z}\\right)\n\\]\n\nGiven \\((x, y, z) = (2, -1, -2)\\), we calculate:\n\\[\n\\rho = \\sqrt{2^2 + (-1)^2 + (-2)^2} = \\sqrt{4 + 1 + 4} = \\sqrt{9} = 3\n\\]\n\nNext, we find \\(\\theta\\):\n\\[\n\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{-1}{2}\\right)\n\\]\n\\(\\theta\\) is in the fourth quadrant because \\(x > 0\\) and \\(y < 0\\).\n\n\\[\n\\phi = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2^2 + (-1)^2}}{-2}\\right) = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{-2}\\right)\n\\]\n\\(\\phi\\) is in the third quadrant because \\(z < 0\\).\n\n### Step 2: Determine the New Spherical Coordinates\n\nThe new spherical coordinates are \\((\\rho, \\theta, 2\\phi)\\). Since \\(\\rho\\) and \\(\\theta\\) remain the same, we only need to find \\(2\\phi\\).\n\n### Step 3: Convert New Spherical Coordinates Back to Rectangular Coordinates\n\nThe formulas to convert from spherical coordinates \\((\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)\\) to rectangular coordinates \\((x, y, z)\\) are:\n\\[\nx = \\rho \\sin \\phi \\cos \\theta\n\\]\n\\[\ny = \\rho \\sin \\phi \\sin \\theta\n\\]\n\\[\nz = \\rho \\cos \\phi\n\\]\n\n","prefix_end":1583,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":false,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[1249,11625,279,3491,11,582,1184,