872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,32,1459,702,51424,13934,4930,17,4999,16,4999,17,15087,323,64151,13934,4930,59,40322,11,1124,15976,11,1124,16827,568,3,220,7379,279,51424,13934,315,279,1459,448,64151,13934,4930,59,40322,11,1124,15976,11,220,17,1124,16827,568,3,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nA point has rectangular coordinates $(2,-1,-2)$ and spherical coordinates $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi).$  Find the rectangular coordinates of the point with spherical coordinates $(\\rho, \\theta, 2 \\phi).$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"68ca1e186cdb791d3f044f59ed1b96b72cca9ac5e9c976a964229341f373a993"},"seconds":31.49297571182251,"seed":119180755251357183,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Convert the given rectangular coordinates \\((2, -1, -2)\\) to spherical coordinates \\((\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)\\).\n2. Determine the new spherical coordinates \\((\\rho, \\theta, 2\\phi)\\).\n3. Convert the new spherical coordinates back to rectangular coordinates.\n\n### Step 1: Convert Rectangular Coordinates to Spherical Coordinates\n\nThe formulas to convert from rectangular coordinates \\((x, y, z)\\) to spherical coordinates \\((\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)\\) are:\n\\[\n\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\phi = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}}{z}\\right)\n\\]\n\nGiven \\((x, y, z) = (2, -1, -2)\\), we calculate:\n\\[\n\\rho = \\sqrt{2^2 + (-1)^2 + (-2)^2} = \\sqrt{4 + 1 + 4} = \\sqrt{9} = 3\n\\]\n\nNext, we find \\(\\theta\\):\n\\[\n\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{-1}{2}\\right)\n\\]\n\\(\\theta\\) is in the fourth quadrant because \\(x > 0\\) and \\(y < 0\\).\n\n\\[\n\\phi = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2^2 + (-1)^2}}{-2}\\right) = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{-2}\\right)\n\\]\n\\(\\phi\\) is in the third quadrant because \\(z < 0\\).\n\n### Step 2: Determine the New Spherical Coordinates\n\nThe new spherical coordinates are \\((\\rho, \\theta, 2\\phi)\\). Since \\(\\rho\\) and \\(\\theta\\) remain the same, we only need to find \\(2\\phi\\).\n\n### Step 3: Convert New Spherical Coordinates Back to Rectangular Coordinates\n\nThe formulas to convert from spherical coordinates \\((\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)\\) to rectangular coordinates \\((x, y, z)\\) are:\n\\[\nx = \\rho \\sin \\phi \\cos \\theta\n\\]\n\\[\ny = \\rho \\sin \\phi \\sin \\theta\n\\]\n\\[\nz = \\rho \\cos \\phi\n\\]\n\nFor the new coordinates \\((\\rho, \\theta, 2\\phi)\\):\n\\[\nx = \\rho \\sin (2\\phi) \\cos \\theta\n\\]\n\\[\ny = \\rho \\sin (2\\phi) \\sin \\theta\n\\]\n\\[\nz = \\rho \\cos (2\\phi)\n\\]\n\nUsing the double-angle identities:\n\\[\n\\sin (2\\phi) = 2 \\sin \\phi \\cos \\phi\n\\]\n\\[\n\\cos (2\\phi) = \\cos^2 \\phi - \\sin^2 \\phi\n\\]\n\nFirst, we need \\(\\sin \\phi\\) and \\(\\cos \\phi\\):\n\\[\n\\cos \\phi = \\frac{z}{\\rho} = \\frac{-2}{3}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sin \\phi = \\sqrt{1 - \\cos^2 \\phi} = \\sqrt{1 - \\left(\\frac{-2}{3}\\right)^2} = \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{4}{9}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{5}{9}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\n\\]\n\nNow, calculate \\(\\sin (2\\phi)\\) and \\(\\cos (2\\phi)\\):\n\\[\n\\sin (2\\phi) = 2 \\sin \\phi \\cos \\phi = 2 \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} \\cdot \\frac{-2}{3} = \\frac{-4\\sqrt{5}}{9}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\cos (2\\phi) = \\cos^2 \\phi - \\sin^2 \\phi = \\left(\\frac{-2}{3}\\right)^2 - \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\right)^2 = \\frac{4}{9} - \\frac{5}{9} = -\\frac{1}{9}\n\\]\n\nFinally, substitute these values back into the rectangular coordinates formulas:\n\\[\nx = \\rho \\sin (2\\phi) \\cos \\theta = 3 \\cdot \\frac{-4\\sqrt{5}}{9} \\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} = 3 \\cdot \\frac{-4\\sqrt{5}}{9} \\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} = 3 \\cdot \\frac{-8}{9} = -\\frac{8}{3}\n\\]\n\\[\ny = \\rho \\sin (2\\phi) \\sin \\theta = 3 \\cdot \\frac{-4\\sqrt{5}}{9} \\cdot \\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{5}} = 3 \\cdot \\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{9} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} = 3 \\cdot \\frac{4}{9} = \\frac{4}{3}\n\\]\n\\[\nz = \\rho \\cos (2\\phi) = 3 \\cdot -\\frac{1}{9} = -\\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the rectangular coordinates of the point with spherical coordinates \\((\\rho, \\theta, 2\\phi)\\) are:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left(-\\frac{8}{3}, \\frac{4}{3}, -\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\left(-\\frac{8}{3}, \\frac{4}{3}, -\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"precalculus:336"}
